    "q-94279": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94279",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants.",
      "worked_solution": "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)$\n\nTherefore, only statement III could be used to represent  $y(t).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
