      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "A particle of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=30\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+6y=0$\n\nII. The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n\nIII. The general solution can be expressed as $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t+ϕ),$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{30}{5}=6\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n1\\. $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n2\\. $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n3\\. $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\sqrt{6}$ and ${ϕ}_{1}=ϕ$ in form 3 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
